>>>markdown
# Eval run

One or more scored runs over one evaluation set. Publisher renders this natively,
so every table below is Malloy reading the same model the app reads -- there is no
second copy of the logic to drift.

This notebook is the **analytical** half: how the arms did, what it cost, and what
to fix next. The **case-by-case** half lives in the app at `/public/index.html`,
because reading an eval is mostly drilling into one case and a notebook cannot
open a drawer.

Read it top to bottom: the headline, then whether the headline is trustworthy,
then where the failures are, then what to do about them.
>>>malloy
import 'eval_run.malloy'
>>>markdown
## 1. The headline

Pass rate over **decided** verdicts -- `match` against `no_match`. Two things are
excluded rather than counted as failures, because a verdict the judge would not
stand behind is not evidence either way and burying it in the denominator
quietly depresses every arm:

- `needs_human`, where the judge had too little confidence to say.
- `near_match`, which means *defensibly different* rather than nearly right.

Read `confident_count` alongside the rate: two arms are only comparable when they
answered the same cases. Read `near_matches` too. It is the leading indicator of
rubrics going vague, and it moves before the pass rate does.
>>>malloy
run: run_summary
>>>markdown
## 2. Is the headline trustworthy?

A pass rate means nothing if the attempts behind it were contaminated, if the
harness fell over, or if the arms did not face the same set.

Any contamination at all voids the run -- an attempt that saw the gold answer is
not evidence about the model. Harness errors are excluded from the rate rather
than counted as failures: a timeout is the eval failing, not the answerer.
>>>malloy
run: attempts -> {
  group_by: arm is runs.label
  aggregate:
    attempt_count
    submitted_count
    contaminated_count
    harness_errors
    judge_version is runs.judge_version.max()
    set_version is runs.set_version.max()
}
>>>markdown
## 3. What it cost

The claim a semantic model makes is not only that the agent gets the right
answer, but that it gets there quickly: fewer turns, fewer tokens, less money.
An arm that wins on accuracy while costing three times as much has not obviously
won.
>>>malloy
run: cost
>>>malloy
run: effort
>>>markdown
## 4. Where the failures are

Every failure carries exactly one **where to fix**, so these counts sum to the
run's failure count. If they ever do not, the attribution has a hole and the two
tables below disagree with each other.

- **query construction** -- retrieval delivered everything; the agent wrote the
  query wrong. Fix the skill.
- **retrieval ranking** -- the entity exists and search did not return it. Fix
  ranking.
- **model coverage** -- there was nothing to return. Add the entity.
- **refusal behaviour** -- the model genuinely cannot answer, and the agent
  answered anyway instead of declining.
>>>malloy
run: failures_by_where_to_fix
>>>markdown
### Pass rate by coverage

Coverage is the ceiling on what any agent can do. A model-shaped problem shows up
here as `derivable` scoring far below `covered`: the answer was reachable, but
only by assembling it, and the agent could not.

`absent` cases are scored on the **refusal** -- passing means the agent correctly
declined.
>>>malloy
run: pass_by_coverage
>>>markdown
## 5. Retrieval

**Per-question entity recall**: of the entities each golden answer depends on, how
many the agent's own `get_context` calls delivered -- as a ranked entity, under a
sibling source, or named in a returned source's documentation. It uses the search
terms the answerer chose, so it moves when the answerer changes; use it to
attribute a failure *within* one arm, not to compare retrieval across arms.
>>>malloy
run: retrieval_as_used
>>>markdown
### Entities most often needed and not returned

The ranked retrieval backlog. An entity high on this list is one fix that several
cases are waiting on.
>>>malloy
run: most_missed_entities
>>>markdown
## 6. What to do next

Failures grouped by shared root cause, ordered by how many cases one edit would
clear. Eight failures are rarely eight problems.

`proposal` is **empty until someone decides what to change**. A cluster with no
proposal is an open question, and that is an honest state -- a generated proposal
that nobody stands behind is worse than a blank, because a reviewer has to
disprove it.
>>>malloy
run: backlog
>>>markdown
### Coverage gaps in the set itself

Independent of any run: cases the model cannot answer outright, with the entity
that would close the gap. This is the model-improvement backlog even before an
agent has attempted anything, and it is the part a customer can act on directly.
>>>malloy
run: coverage_gaps
>>>markdown
## 7. Refusal behaviour

Refusal is scored in both directions, and getting one right while getting the
other wrong is a common and misleading result.

- A case whose golden is **unanswerable** passes by declining and naming what is
  missing. Substituting a proxy without saying so is a failure.
- A case tagged **answerable-sounds-unanswerable** passes by *answering*. An agent
  that has learned to refuse whenever a question sounds hard will fail these while
  looking well-calibrated on the ones above.
>>>malloy
run: refusal_behaviour
>>>markdown
## 8. Comparing arms

Only meaningful with two or more arms loaded.

A difference in pass **rate** is not evidence. Two arms can post the same rate
while disagreeing on a third of the set, and a two-point gap over 49 cases is
one case. What matters is which cases actually moved, and in which direction.

`arms_passed` between 1 and `arms` means the arms disagree. Read the nested
verdicts to see who won each one.
>>>malloy
run: flips
>>>markdown
### Cases every arm failed

Changing the answerer will not move these. They are where model and retrieval
work pays off, and they are the most durable finding in the whole run.
>>>malloy
run: failed_everywhere
